<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Our Lady of Vladimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Theotokos of Vladimir, Virgin of Vladimir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Our Lady of Vladimir is a Byzantine icon of the Virgin and Child, painted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the early twelfth century and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> around 1130. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> took it to Vladimir in 1155, and in 1395 it was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where it was credited with saving the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>. One of the most venerated images in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tretyakov-gallery">Tretyakov Gallery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir.jpg" alt="Our Lady of Vladimir" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel Bakni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_of_Vladimir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The icon is a tempera painting on wood, 106 by 69 centimetres, by an unknown artist, most likely working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and is an early example of the Eleusa type, in which mother and child embrace cheek to cheek. It reached Rus' in the twelfth century, which fits the accounts in the Rus' chronicles. The Greek Patriarch of Constantinople sent it as a gift to Grand Prince Yuri Dolgorukiy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and the scholar Sona Hoisington connects this in part with Byzantine efforts to convert the Slavic peoples. It stayed in a nunnery at Vyshhorod until Yuri's son Andrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogolyubovo">Bogolyubovo</a> took it to Vladimir in 1155.</p><p>A traditional account says that the horses carrying it stopped near Vladimir and refused to go on, which people took as a sign that the Mother of God wished it to stay. Andrey built the Assumption Cathedral to support his claim that Vladimir had replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> as the principal city of Rus, and the icon was moved there after the consecration in 1160.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The icon was damaged in a fire in 1195 and again when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> burned Vladimir in 1238. It has been restored at least five times, including in 1431 and 1512, and only the faces and the gold ground above Mary's head are original. According to tradition it was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1395 during Tamerlane's invasion, and his armies withdrew the same day. David Miller suggests that it normally remained in Vladimir until the 1520s, and notes that the story of its saving Moscow does not appear in sources until the late 15th century.</p><p>From the 16th century it became a symbol in legends linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to pre-Mongol Rus, first to support the imperial claims of Ivan IV. It has been used in the coronations of tsars and the elections of patriarchs, and has three feast days, on 3 June, 6 July and 8 September. After the Revolution it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tretyakov-gallery">State Tretyakov Gallery</a>, which first displayed it in 1930. In the 1990s ownership was disputed between the gallery and the Moscow Patriarchate, and it was placed in the Church of St. Nicholas in Tolmachi, which serves as both a house church and part of the museum. Art historian David Talbot Rice called it one of the most outstanding religious paintings of the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virgin of Vladimir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The icon of <b>Our Lady of Vladimir</b>, painted in Constantinople in the early 12th century, was brought north by Andrei Bogolyubsky in 1155 and is now in Moscow’s Tretyakov Gallery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
